A||||10||Standard||i hate ball joints...||||8||
Z||000000||Standard||03-09-2000||01:44 AM||||ok gang...<BR>after hammering on the front lower balljoint for forever and a day, i gave up and decided to to take the a arm off and bring it to a shop to have them press it out and press the new one in.  <BR>Trouble is, I can't get the pivot bolts out!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> i got the nuts off both of them, but the bolts are stuck to the bushing.  They're soaking in wd-40 right now.. anybody have any suggestions before I take the torch to it?<BR>freakin ball joints...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">||||reg||8
Z||000001||Oreif||03-09-2000||02:01 AM||||I just redid my entire front end. The best way to do it without a torch is to move the A-arm down, Put a ratchet on the bolt, Spray WD-40 all around it. Then spin bolt, If bolt is frozen to the sleeve, Hit the threaded side with a hammer then try to spin it. I had to do this for about 20 minutes and it finally came off. The thing that really is bad is the front lower A-arm bolt. Some Fieros have it where you need to drop the rack to get the bolt out! (mine did) Once you get the bolts out just get new ones. I replaced all my bushings with poly. Also make sure the new ball joints have the safety ring clip. It took me an entire weekend to tear apart the whole front end and a day to re-assemble it.<BR>Good Luck!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||03-09-2000||02:12 AM||||Thanks Orief! didn't have to tell me it took you a whole weekend to get it apart, tho.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> had been doing that exact thing with my dad for quite some time.  The front side bolt refuses to move.  It'll spin, but it won't go anywhere.  We got the back bolt out a good ways, but not all the way.  <BR>This is on the drivers side, and with the wheel all the way to the right it looks like there will be enough room to clear the rack. actually only the tie rod is kind of in the way.  Still enough space to put a socket with an extension on it. <BR>Got moog ball joints 'cause I know batty likes 'em!  saw the safety clip in there.<BR>Might just get to the point where we take the good ole' sawz-all to the #$@#% things.  Think the back bolt is a little bent anyways.. <BR>thanks for any and all help! ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||03-09-2000||07:19 AM||||You might try an auto parts store some of the larger chains have free loaner tools. I made a contraption with some large sockets and big "C" clamps crude but it worked. Remember that there should be a tack weld from the factory on the unit be sure to cut it. Of course I'm speaking of the ball joint not the A frame.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Phil (edited 03-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||03-09-2000||07:46 AM||||I tightly clamped a pair of vice-grips (one of my favorite tools) onto the head side of the bolt. By twisting the bolt back and forth, try to either tap on the nut end of the bolt or on the side of the vice-grips with a micro adjusting tools (hammer). The head of the front bolt will hit the steering stabilizer, I just loosened my up a little and was able to slide the bolt past it with a minimum of prying. If the rear bolt is out, you can tip the back of the cotrol arm up a bit to allow more clearance. If you do resort to cutting the bolt off, make sure you replace it with a grade 8 hardened bolt, not some wimpy regular soft bolt. Good luck, mine put up a bit of a struggle, but it sounds like yours is fighting to the death.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mwbackus||03-09-2000||10:10 AM||||Why not just rent a ball joint press?  Then you can easily remove and install the joints with the arm on the car.  If there is an Autozone in your area, I know they rent them for FREE(just need a deposit). Problem solved.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||03-09-2000||10:44 AM||||Mike, there's no going back to do it the easy way now. It's become a matter of pride now! Standard <B>WILL</B> win the battle of the control arm bolts! (Hahaha)<P>Yeah, I forgot about the portable ball joint presses you can rent. I wanted to change my bushings at the same time, so I had to remove my control arms. Don't give up Standard, ya almost got it licked!<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 03-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||03-09-2000||02:26 PM||||There's no Autozone around here, and the only place the has a balljoint press to rent wants $30 to rent it and a $200 deposit...  anyways, like BB said, I can't go back to the easy way now!  too bad the temp. up here dropped about 30* in one day, and we now have snow on the ground.. <BR>time to go beat on my car some more.. thanks everyone!||||reg||1
Z||000008||deceler8||03-09-2000||03:03 PM||||You can buy a ball joint press from Harbor Freight Tools for $29.95.<P>I did...now waiting for warmer weather to use it.<P>Anyone who has used one...does it easily remove the welded-on fronts or are other steps required ?||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||03-09-2000||04:01 PM||||The front lower Fiero ball joints are pressed in. Some of them apparently have a factory spot weld or two tacked on to secure them. None of the Fieros I've worked on had these welds. If yours does, you need to break the spot weld first with a chisel and hammer before pressing them out.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Bob Englert||03-10-2000||11:28 PM||||On my 85 SE last year I had to cut 2 of the lower control arm bolts off with a recipricating saw.  The bolts were completely frozen to the innder sleeve of the bushing.  I think I went through about 6 saw blades to get them completely out.  I was able to get replacement bolts from Pontiac.  It was definetely not fun but worth the effort, the car feels great now.||||reg||1
